    ('What does decimal.Context() do?', '`decimal.Context()`: The context affects almost all operations and controls rounding, Over/Underflow, raising of exceptions and much more. A new context can be constructed as follows:'),
    ('What does decimal.Decimal() do?', "`decimal.Decimal()`: Construct a new Decimal object. 'value' can be an integer, string, tuple, or another Decimal object. If no value is given, return Decimal('0'). The context does not affect the conversion and is only passed to determine if the InvalidOperation trap is active."),
    ('What does decimal.DecimalTuple() do?', '`decimal.DecimalTuple()`: Stores a Decimal value as its sign, digit tuple, and exponent.'),
    ('What does fractions.Fraction() do?', '`fractions.Fraction()`: This class implements rational numbers.'),
    ('What does operator.attrgetter() do?', "`operator.attrgetter()`: Return a callable object that fetches the given attribute(s) from its operand. After f = attrgetter('name'), the call f(r) returns r.name. After g = attrgetter('name', 'date'), the call g(r) returns (r.name, r.date). After h = attrgetter('name.first', 'name.last'), the call h(r) returns (r.name.first, r.name.last)."),
    ('What does operator.itemgetter() do?', '`operator.itemgetter()`: Return a callable object that fetches the given item(s) from its operand. After f = itemgetter(2), the call f(r) returns r[2]. After g = itemgetter(2, 5, 3), the call g(r) returns (r[2], r[5], r[3])'),
    ('What does operator.methodcaller() do?', "`operator.methodcaller()`: Return a callable object that calls the given method on its operand. After f = methodcaller('name'), the call f(r) returns r.name(). After g = methodcaller('name', 'date', foo=1), the call g(r) returns r.name('date', foo=1)."),
    ('What does copy.copy() do?', '`copy.copy()`: Shallow copy operation on arbitrary Python objects.'),
    ('What does copy.deepcopy() do?', '`copy.deepcopy()`: Deep copy operation on arbitrary Python objects.'),
    ('What does copy.replace() do?', '`copy.replace()`: Return a new object replacing specified fields with new values. Available in Python 3.13 and newer.'),
    ('What does contextlib.AbstractAsyncContextManager() do?', '`contextlib.AbstractAsyncContextManager()`: An abstract base class for asynchronous context managers.'),
    ('What does contextlib.AbstractContextManager() do?', '`contextlib.AbstractContextManager()`: An abstract base class for context managers.'),
    ('What does contextlib.AsyncContextDecorator() do?', '`contextlib.AsyncContextDecorator()`: A base class or mixin that enables async context managers to work as decorators.'),
    ('What does contextlib.AsyncExitStack() do?', '`contextlib.AsyncExitStack()`: Async context manager for dynamic management of a stack of exit callbacks.'),
    ('What does contextlib.ContextDecorator() do?', '`contextlib.ContextDecorator()`: A base class or mixin that enables context managers to work as decorators.'),
    ('What does contextlib.ExitStack() do?', '`contextlib.ExitStack()`: Context manager for dynamic management of a stack of exit callbacks.'),
    ('What does contextlib.aclosing() do?', '`contextlib.aclosing()`: Async context manager for safely finalizing an asynchronously cleaned-up resource such as an async generator, calling its ``aclose()`` method.'),
    ('What does contextlib.asynccontextmanager() do?', '`contextlib.asynccontextmanager()`: @asynccontextmanager decorator.'),
    ('What does contextlib.chdir() do?', '`contextlib.chdir()`: Non thread-safe context manager to change the current working directory.'),
    ('What does contextlib.closing() do?', '`contextlib.closing()`: Context to automatically close something at the end of a block.'),
    ('What does contextlib.contextmanager() do?', '`contextlib.contextmanager()`: @contextmanager decorator.'),
    ('What does contextlib.nullcontext() do?', '`contextlib.nullcontext()`: Context manager that does no additional processing.'),
    ('What does contextlib.redirect_stderr() do?', '`contextlib.redirect_stderr()`: Context manager for temporarily redirecting stderr to another file.'),
    ('What does contextlib.redirect_stdout() do?', '`contextlib.redirect_stdout()`: Context manager for temporarily redirecting stdout to another file.'),
    ('What does contextlib.suppress() do?', '`contextlib.suppress()`: Context manager to suppress specified exceptions'),
    ('What does enum.Enum() do?', '`enum.Enum()`: Create a collection of name/value pairs.'),
    ('What does enum.EnumCheck() do?', '`enum.EnumCheck()`: various conditions to check an enumeration for'),
    ('What does enum.EnumDict() do?', '`enum.EnumDict()`: Track enum member order and ensure member names are not reused. Available in Python 3.13 and newer.'),
    ('What does enum.EnumMeta() do?', '`enum.EnumMeta()`: Metaclass for Enum'),
    ('What does enum.EnumType() do?', '`enum.EnumType()`: Metaclass for Enum'),
    ('What does enum.Flag() do?', '`enum.Flag()`: Support for flags'),
    ('What does enum.FlagBoundary() do?', '`enum.FlagBoundary()`: control how out of range values are handled "strict" -> error is raised [default for Flag] "conform" -> extra bits are discarded "eject" -> lose flag status "keep" -> keep flag status and all bits [default for IntFlag]'),
    ('What does enum.IntEnum() do?', '`enum.IntEnum()`: Enum where members are also (and must be) ints'),
